# NitroLite — Linux permissions
#
# The Acer hardware settings are sysfs attributes owned by root and created 0644, so a normal user can read them but
# not write them. This rule hands exactly those attributes to the "nitrolite" group, which NitroLite's Settings page
# (or install.sh) creates and adds you to. Without it NitroLite still runs: telemetry works, the operating mode goes
# through power-profiles-daemon, and every other control says what is missing.
#
# Install (the Settings page's "Enable" button does all of this behind one password prompt):
#   sudo groupadd -f nitrolite && sudo usermod -aG nitrolite "$USER"
#   sudo install -m 644 99-nitrolite.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
#   sudo udevadm control --reload-rules && sudo udevadm trigger
# Then log out and back in, so the session picks up the group.
#
# Why group ownership and not TAG+="uaccess": uaccess makes logind put an ACL on a device's /dev node. A sysfs attribute
# has no /dev node, so the tag never reached the files NitroLite writes. It is still the right tool for the two real
# device nodes here, the keyboard controller's /dev/hidrawN and the Nitro key's /dev/input/eventN.
#
# Every change is 0664/g+w to the nitrolite group: readable by everyone as the kernel left it, writable by the group,
# never by everyone. A node that lost its read bit could be written through the helper and never read back. If the group
# does not exist the chgrp fails and the attribute keeps the mode the kernel gave it, which is the safe direction to
# fail in.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Global attributes, applied when the acer-wmi platform device appears at boot
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# These rules match every event of the acer-wmi platform device, not only "add". That matters: the kernel announces the
# device ("add") before the driver has probed it, and it is the probe that registers the platform profile. The "bind"
# event that follows the probe runs the same rules again, by which time the attribute exists.
#
# Known limit: the processor boost switch belongs to the cpufreq driver, not to acer-wmi. Where that driver is a module
# that loads after acer-wmi (acpi-cpufreq on some kernels), neither event finds the switch and it stays root-only until
# the next "udevadm trigger" or reboot. A failed chgrp is silent here by design (the rule must not fail the event). The
# NitroLite helper (installed by the .deb/.rpm and install.sh --system) is the primary write path for both attributes
# and does not depend on this timing; these rules are the fallback for installs without it.

# The ACPI platform profile (the operating mode). It lives under /sys/firmware/acpi/, which has no device of its own.
SUBSYSTEM=="platform", KERNEL=="acer-wmi", RUN+="/bin/sh -c 'PATH=/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin; chgrp nitrolite /sys/firmware/acpi/platform_profile 2>/dev/null && chmod 0664 /sys/firmware/acpi/platform_profile; true'"

# The processor boost switch school mode turns off: cpufreq/boost on AMD and most Intel, intel_pstate/no_turbo on Intel
# in active mode. Only the node the kernel actually has is touched.
SUBSYSTEM=="platform", KERNEL=="acer-wmi", RUN+="/bin/sh -c 'PATH=/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin; for f in /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpufreq/boost /sys/devices/system/cpu/intel_pstate/no_turbo; do [ -e $$f ] && chgrp nitrolite $$f 2>/dev/null && chmod 0664 $$f; done; true'"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The in-kernel acer-wmi driver
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Fan control is deliberately NOT handed to the group. pwm1/pwm2 accept 0, which stops a fan, and nothing on a group
# write puts the fans back in automatic when the app that set them dies. Fan writes go through the NitroLite helper,
# which refuses a speed below the app's floor and hands the fans back to the firmware when its client disconnects.

# The battery charge limit: the standard threshold where the battery class has one, and the charge type that the
# in-kernel acer-wmi-battery driver (Linux 7.3) uses for Acer's 80 % health mode.
SUBSYSTEM=="power_supply", ATTR{type}=="Battery", RUN+="/bin/sh -c 'PATH=/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin; cd /sys$env{DEVPATH} && for f in charge_control_end_threshold charge_control_start_threshold charge_types; do [ -e $$f ] && chgrp nitrolite $$f 2>/dev/null && chmod 0664 $$f; done; true'"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The out-of-tree modules (Linuwu-Sense, acer-wmi-linuwu)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Their settings sit on the acer-wmi platform device in nitro_sense/ or predator_sense/, with the keyboard in
# four_zoned_kb/ beside them (or below them, on older builds). Only attributes the owner may already write are widened,
# so a read-only attribute stays read-only for everyone. fan_speed is left to the helper, for the reason above.
SUBSYSTEM=="platform", KERNEL=="acer-wmi", RUN+="/bin/sh -c 'PATH=/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin; for d in /sys$env{DEVPATH}/nitro_sense /sys$env{DEVPATH}/predator_sense /sys$env{DEVPATH}/four_zoned_kb /sys$env{DEVPATH}/nitro_sense/four_zoned_kb /sys$env{DEVPATH}/predator_sense/four_zoned_kb; do [ -d $$d ] && find $$d -maxdepth 1 -type f -perm -u+w ! -name fan_speed -exec chgrp nitrolite {} + -exec chmod g+w {} + 2>/dev/null; done; true'"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A real device node
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The ENE KB5130 keyboard controller on the 2024-and-later Nitro V, Nitro 16S and Helios Neo boards. The colour is an
# HID feature report written to /dev/hidrawN; uaccess gives the user at the seat that one node. No other HID device is
# matched.
#
# Two rules because the controller sits on two buses. On USB the parent chain carries idVendor/idProduct. On I2C-HID
# (Nitro V 16 ANV16-I31) it carries neither, so that one is matched on the HID device's own kernel name,
# <bus>:<vendor>:<product>.<instance> with bus 0018 = I2C.
#
# The uaccess builtin is run here as well as the tag being set: systemd applies the tag from 73-seat-late.rules, which
# has already been read by the time this file is, so the tag alone only takes effect at the next session change. The
# tag is still needed: it is what logind uses to move the ACL when the seat's user changes. Not on remove: the node is
# gone by then and the builtin would only log that it could not find it.
ACTION!="remove", SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ATTRS{idVendor}=="0cf2", ATTRS{idProduct}=="5130", TAG+="uaccess", RUN{builtin}+="uaccess"
ACTION!="remove", SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", KERNELS=="0018:0CF2:5130.*", TAG+="uaccess", RUN{builtin}+="uaccess"

# The Nitro key. acer-wmi reports it on its own input device, "Acer WMI hotkeys", which carries the laptop's hotkeys and
# nothing typed. Only that one event node is handed to the user at the seat, the same way as the keyboard controller
# above. This replaces adding the user to the "input" group, which would let every program of that user read every
# keyboard and mouse on the machine. Installs that already added the group keep it; "sudo gpasswd -d $USER input"
# removes it once this rule is in place.
ACTION!="remove", SUBSYSTEM=="input", KERNEL=="event*", ATTRS{name}=="Acer WMI hotkeys", TAG+="uaccess", RUN{builtin}+="uaccess"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# If the operating mode still says "permission denied"
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NitroLite switches the mode through power-profiles-daemon (or tuned-ppd) when it cannot write the attribute, which
# needs no permission at all:
#     sudo systemctl enable --now power-profiles-daemon
# The Settings page says which of the two paths it is using.
